<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Analects</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Analects of Confucius, Lunyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Analects (Lunyu) is a collection of sayings and short dialogues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> and his disciples, compiled by later followers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and fixed in its present form in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. Its twenty books set out the ideals of humaneness, ritual propriety, filial piety and rule by moral example. It became the central text of Confucian education across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/analects.jpg" alt="Analects" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Confucius and his disciples · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rongo_Analects_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Ban Gu, writing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a>, the Analects began as individual records kept by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s disciples of their conversations with him, which they collected and jointly edited after his death in 479 BCE. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> the work was titled Lunyu, which may mean edited conversations or selected speeches. Scholars agree that large portions were composed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and that the work reached its final form in the mid-Han dynasty.</p><p>Liu Xiang records that two versions existed at the beginning of the Han: the Lu version of twenty chapters and the Qi version of twenty-two. A third Old Text version, written in characters not used since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, was found hidden in a wall of a house believed to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s. The received text is mostly based on the Lu version.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The chapters are grouped by theme but do not form a continuous argument, and adjacent chapters are often unrelated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> describes himself as a transmitter of the traditions of the early Zhou, not an originator. The central idea is ren, an all-encompassing state of virtue that, in the text, no living person had completely attained. Asked to define it, Confucius responds indirectly, with examples, and the basic step towards it is filial piety.</p><p>In politics the text holds that a good ruler is self-disciplined and governs by education and example rather than by punishment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> blames social chaos on rulers who claim titles they do not deserve. Education is a fundamental theme, and he is traditionally credited with teaching three thousand students, seventy of whom mastered what he taught. The Analects is arguably the most reliable source for his biography.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During most of the Han period the Analects was not a principal text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a> were canonical, and the Analects was treated as a collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s commentary on them. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> it was widely read by schoolchildren. In the late Song dynasty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> promoted it as one of the Four Books, and it came to be regarded as more insightful than the older Five Classics.</p><p>He Yan's Collected Explanations, written in 248 CE, is the oldest complete commentary still extant and remained the standard guide for nearly a thousand years. The Analects has been among the most widely read books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for more than two thousand years, and its ideas still influence East Asian thought and values.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Analects” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japanese chronicles relate that the scholar Wani came from Baekje bringing the <b>Analects</b> and, anachronistically, the Thousand Character Classic, and that King Seong sent a Buddhist image and sutras to the Yamato court, in 552 according to the Nihon shoki, though another Japanese source gives 538, the date…</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…hegemony created a model in which a powerful lord protected the Zhou order and the Chinese states against outside peoples, and Confucius praised Guan Zhong for it, saying in the <b>Analects</b> that without him people would be wearing their hair loose and folding their robes to the left.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He crushed the general Ren Dejing when Ren demanded half the realm in 1170, and made the Confucian Wo Daochong, translator of the <b>Analects</b> into Tangut, chief minister.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His influence came through his students, said to number seventy-two close disciples and some three thousand in all, who recorded his sayings and conversations; these were compiled over the following generations into the <b>Analects</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Classical Chinese is that of the great texts of the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, from the 6th to the 3rd centuries BCE: the <b>Analects of Confucius</b>, the Mencius, the Zhuangzi, the Xunzi, the Han Feizi and the Zuo Commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>China's oldest songs reach us through anthologies: Guan ju, the first poem of the Classic of Poetry, which Confucius praised in the <b>Analects</b>, and the Song of the Yue Boatman, its original sounds transcribed in 32 Chinese characters.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li></ul></section>
